Source: sonic
Maintainer: SONiC Team <sonic-dev@lists.sonicfoundation.dev>
Section: net
Priority: optional
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
                libswsscommon-dev,
                libyang-dev,
                python3-dev
Standards-Version: 1.0.0

Package: sonic-thermalctld-rs
Architecture: any
# sonic-platform-common is deliberately not here: it is a Python wheel the
# image pip-installs, not a Debian package, so dpkg could never satisfy it --
# and an unsatisfiable Depends makes `apt-get install -f` want to remove this
# package, which the pmon Dockerfile forbids with --no-remove.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, python3
Description: SONiC thermal control daemon, in Rust
 Rust port of thermalctld.  Writes the same TEMPERATURE_INFO, FAN_INFO,
 FAN_DRAWER_INFO and PHYSICAL_ENTITY_INFO entries as the Python
 implementation.
 .
 It reaches the platform through the generated cross-language facade in
 sonic-platform-common, which calls whichever vendor's Python
 sonic_platform package the image installs -- so this one binary runs on
 any platform that has one, and no vendor has to change anything to get it.
 .
 The binary embeds a CPython interpreter to make those calls.  A later
 phase replaces the bridge with a native Rust implementation of the same
 trait, which removes the interpreter without touching the daemon.

Package: sonic-psud-rs
Architecture: any
# sonic-platform-common is deliberately not here: it is a Python wheel the
# image pip-installs, not a Debian package, so dpkg could never satisfy it --
# and an unsatisfiable Depends makes `apt-get install -f` want to remove this
# package, which the pmon Dockerfile forbids with --no-remove.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, python3
Description: SONiC PSU monitoring daemon, in Rust
 Rust port of psud.  Writes the same PSU_INFO, the PSU half of FAN_INFO,
 the PSU tray LEDs and -- on a modular chassis -- the power budget in
 CHASSIS_INFO entries as the Python implementation.
 .
 It reaches the platform through the generated cross-language facade in
 sonic-platform-common, which calls whichever vendor's Python
 sonic_platform package the image installs -- so this one binary runs on
 any platform that has one, and no vendor has to change anything to get it.
 .
 The binary embeds a CPython interpreter to make those calls.  A later
 phase replaces the bridge with a native Rust implementation of the same
 trait, which removes the interpreter without touching the daemon.
